## Technical Patent Summary

### Title  
**Adjustable Feed-Network Phase Delay for a Guided Surface Waveguide System**

### Technical Field  
The disclosure relates to guided electromagnetic-wave systems and, more particularly, to apparatuses and methods for adjusting phase delay within a feed network that excites one or more guided surface-wave modes along a propagation surface.

### Technical Problem  
Variations in operating frequency, feed-line length, dielectric properties, component tolerances, environmental conditions, and loading of a propagation surface can produce phase mismatch between excitation signals supplied to launch structures of a guided surface waveguide system. Such mismatch may reduce launching efficiency, alter the desired field distribution, increase unwanted radiation, or impair constructive combination of guided surface waves.

### Summary of the Disclosure  
A guided surface waveguide system includes a propagation surface, one or more launch structures electromagnetically coupled to the propagation surface, and a feed network that supplies radio-frequency excitation signals to the launch structures. An adjustable phase-delay element is disposed in at least one signal path of the feed network. A controller determines a phase condition associated with the feed network or a guided surface wave propagating along the propagation surface and changes the phase-delay element to establish a target relative phase at the launch structure or structures.

The phase-delay element may include a switched transmission-line network, continuously variable phase shifter, tunable reactance network, ferroelectric or semiconductor phase shifter, mechanically adjustable line section, vector modulator, or another controllable delay device. Adjustment may be open-loop, based on predetermined calibration data, or closed-loop, based on sensed voltage, current, phase, power, field strength, reflection coefficient, or propagation characteristics.

---

## Apparatus Claim Summary

A representative apparatus claim may recite:

> **A guided surface waveguide apparatus comprising:**  
> a propagation surface configured to support propagation of a guided electromagnetic surface wave;  
> at least one launch structure electromagnetically coupled to the propagation surface;  
> a feed network having at least one signal path configured to convey an excitation signal from a radio-frequency source to the at least one launch structure;  
> an adjustable phase-delay element coupled in the at least one signal path and configured to vary a phase delay of the excitation signal; and  
> a controller coupled to the adjustable phase-delay element and configured to set the phase delay to produce a target phase condition for excitation of the guided electromagnetic surface wave.

### Potential Apparatus-Claim Features  
Dependent claims may further specify that:

- the system includes first and second launch structures supplied through respective feed-network branches;
- the controller adjusts a relative phase between excitation signals delivered to the launch structures;
- the target phase condition corresponds to constructive excitation of a selected guided surface-wave mode;
- one or more sensors measure phase, amplitude, impedance, reflected power, delivered power, or surface-field intensity;
- the controller performs closed-loop adjustment using sensor feedback;
- the controller selects a phase setting from stored calibration data indexed by frequency, temperature, load, or operating mode;
- the phase-delay element provides discrete delay states or continuous phase adjustment;
- amplitude and phase are jointly controlled;
- the feed network compensates for unequal electrical lengths between feed branches;
- the phase setting is adjusted as a function of operating frequency to provide a substantially constant time delay or a prescribed phase progression;
- the launch structures are arranged at spaced positions along or around the propagation surface;
- the controller minimizes a reflected-wave metric or maximizes a measured guided-wave metric.

---

## Method Claim Summary

A representative method claim may recite:

> **A method of operating a guided surface waveguide system, the method comprising:**  
> generating an excitation signal for launching a guided electromagnetic surface wave along a propagation surface;  
> conveying the excitation signal through a feed network to at least one launch structure coupled to the propagation surface;  
> determining a phase condition associated with the excitation signal, the feed network, or the guided electromagnetic surface wave;  
> adjusting a phase delay in at least one signal path of the feed network based on the determined phase condition; and  
> exciting the at least one launch structure with the phase-adjusted excitation signal to establish a target guided surface-wave condition along the propagation surface.

### Potential Method-Claim Features  
Dependent method claims may include:

- measuring a relative phase between signals supplied to multiple launch structures;
- sensing a surface-wave field at one or more locations on the propagation surface;
- comparing the sensed or calculated phase condition with a target value;
- incrementally changing the phase delay until a performance metric satisfies a threshold;
- sweeping a plurality of phase settings and selecting the setting that maximizes guided-wave power or minimizes reflected power;
- retrieving an initial phase setting from a calibration table;
- compensating for temperature, frequency, component aging, or changes in load coupling;
- adjusting phase independently for multiple feed-network branches;
- maintaining a prescribed phase relationship while changing operating frequency;
- jointly adjusting phase and amplitude to form or steer a desired surface-wave field distribution;
- repeating the determining and adjusting operations during operation of the system.

### Claimed Technical Effect  
The apparatus and method improve control of the phase presented to the launch structure or structures, thereby increasing guided surface-wave launch efficiency, compensating for feed-network and environmental variations, suppressing undesired modes or radiation, and maintaining a desired electromagnetic-field distribution over the propagation surface.
